% ======================================================================
\section*{Before the proofs}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Before the proofs}
% ======================================================================

Every later part of this series opens quickly and stays inside its proofs.
This one does not, on purpose, because this is the part the others are built on, and a foundation is worth a longer look before anything is set on top of it.

I want to say plainly how this object got made, because the how is not separate from what can be trusted in the what.
It did not start as a proof.
It started as two lines of arithmetic, run a few hundred thousand times, and a rule I tried to hold to throughout: a number does not get to stand until it has been produced twice, by two different routes, and the two routes agree.
A claim proved on paper and a claim read off a table are different kinds of claim, and the difference is not a formality.
It is the whole discipline this series calls object, sheet, and entrance, and it was earned the slow way, by being wrong about the boundary between them more than once and fixing it each time rather than smoothing over it.

Two of those corrections are worth naming, because they shaped what this part is willing to say and how carefully it says it.

The first was a habit of language, not of mathematics.
It is easy, faced with an unfamiliar construction, to reach for one of two lazy verdicts: that it must already be known, or that it must be new.
Both are a way of not looking closely.
The honest version takes longer: check what the operators alone force, check it again by hand, and only then ask what it happens to be called elsewhere.
Every identity in this part was checked that way, in that order.
Where the order was reversed even once --- where a name got reached for before the check was finished --- it was a mistake, and it is not repeated here.

The second correction came from depth rather than direction.
The leftover \(\tau(m)\) has a closed-form sum (Proposition~\ref{prop:tausum} below), and at the scale of the sheets in this part it agrees with that closed form to every digit shown.
Run the same walk far past that scale --- hundreds of times deeper, into the millions of hits --- and a naive running total of \(\tau(m)\) quietly disagrees with the \emph{integral} \(\int_1^N\tau\) by an amount that grows with the depth of the run.
The first reading of that gap, kept here only long enough to correct, was that the addition had failed: summing millions of terms into a number many orders of magnitude larger than any one of them loses precision on every step.
It had not.
A Kahan-compensated running total --- carried with a separate correction term meant to catch exactly that kind of loss --- disagrees from the integral by the same amount as the naive total, to every digit tested.
Nothing was lost in the addition.
What was missing was a term in the comparison: the integral is only the leading part of the discrete sum, and the gap between them is not noise but the Euler--Maclaurin correction Proposition~\ref{prop:wedge} names below, a quantity as fixed as \(\tau\) itself and one this series had simply not yet written down.
Once the trapezoidal correction is restored the residual stops growing with \(N\) and settles on \(\gamma/2-\tfrac14\), the constant Proposition~\ref{prop:wedge} derives.
The lesson survives the correction, sharpened rather than softened: a sum this series reports is always the discrete closed form of Proposition~\ref{prop:tausum}, checked against a direct count at a scale small enough for the count itself to be trustworthy --- and never a running total left to grow unchecked against the wrong comparison, which will disagree with almost anything, precision intact.

With that said, the second thing worth doing plainly, before any proof below, is placing this object next to the mathematics it touches, rather than letting a reader wonder.

Almost nothing proved in this part is new.
The harmonic conjugate of Proposition~\ref{prop:harmonic} is classical projective geometry: any involution of a line with two fixed points sends every point to a partner in a fixed cross-ratio of \(-1\) with those two points, a fact that predates this series by roughly two thousand years in spirit and about a century and a half in the specific vocabulary of cross-ratio itself \cite{coxeter1969}.
The two-integer gap structure of \S\ref{sec:twoint} is a special case of a fact conjectured by Steinhaus and proved independently, within a few years of one another, by several mathematicians studying the fractional parts of multiples of an irrational number \cite{sos1958,swierczkowski1958}.
Building a rotation from a shear and a renormalization, rather than calling sine and cosine directly, is a known technique with its own name and its own lineage in digital computing, developed for a navigation computer, for entirely different reasons, decades before this series existed \cite{volder1959}.
Summing a slowly growing quantity and comparing the sum with a smooth integral is Euler and Maclaurin's, older than the notation used here to state it \cite{apostol1976}.
None of that is a discovery, and this series has said so on its first page.

What is offered instead is the object: one generator, refusing to name an angle anywhere inside its own two moves, that produces all four of the results above as consequences of a single walk, measured against one another rather than assembled to order.
Whether that assembly is worth the years this series intends to give it is a claim the reader can weigh against the tables and the proofs that follow --- not against this paragraph.

\vspace{1.2em}
\noindent
\textit{A note.}
Claims of two kinds are kept apart throughout.
A claim proved from the two moves and the count is an identity, true for every admissible kick and every start on the diameter.
A claim that is a count --- a gap, a word, a mean taken over one run --- is reported from a table, and the table is named.
The recorded run cited throughout this part is \(h=0.002\), \(200\,000\) steps, \(127\) crossings.
That run is an example.
A different kick, or a longer run, writes a new table.
It does not revise an identity.
No angle enters the step.
A length that appears only after a table exists may be named as a comparison, and not before.

\vspace{0.7em}
\noindent
\textit{A note on names used here.}
Shear \(T_h\), bind \(N\), step --- the two moves and their composition.
Hit --- a crossing of the diameter the walk began on; the count of hits is \(m\).
Pair --- \(r=m\), \(1/r=1/m\), written on that count.
Lock --- the hit \(m=1\), where the pair meets itself.
Leftover \(\tau\) --- the gap between the arithmetic mean and the geometric mean of a pair.

