% ======================================================================
\section{What is in the operators}
% ======================================================================

Start on the diameter: \((1,0)\).
A heading is a pair of coordinates whose length, after the bind, is \(1\).

\begin{definition}[Shear]
For a kick \(h>0\),
\[
T_h(x,y)=(x-hy,\; y+hx).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Bind]
\[
N(x,y)=\frac{(x,y)}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Step]
\[
\mathrm{step}=N\circ T_h.
\]
\end{definition}

No angle is an argument of \(T_h\) or of \(N\).
The shear is an equal, opposite mix of the two coordinates.
The bind is length.
Read separately, neither move says anything about a turn.
Composed and repeated they produce a heading that comes back to the line it started on.
That return is counted.
It is not named as a turn inside the step --- though it is worth saying, once, that the composition is exactly a rotation by the fixed angle \(\arctan h\), a shear-and-renormalize construction with its own history in the fast computation of trigonometric functions rather than in geometry \cite{volder1959}.
That fact is never used inside the walk.
It is used only to name a comparison, later, once a table exists to compare against.

If the last heading already had length \(1\), the unbound length after the shear is fixed by \(h\) alone:
\[
\bigl|T_h(u)\bigr|=\sqrt{1+h^2}.
\]
The leftover of one kick is therefore \(\sqrt{1+h^2}-1\), constant in the step index.
On the recorded run this is \(1.999998\times 10^{-6}\).
The Euclidean distance between two successive bound headings is likewise a function of \(h\) only.
On the recorded run it is \(0.001999997\).
Those two numbers describe the grain of one run.
They are not a position on the pair.

\begin{observation}
An unequal mix \((x-ay,\, y+bx)\) still returns to the line, but mean gap times kick tracks a different comparison.
A one-sided mix from this start does not return.
The equal opposite shear is a choice of kind.
It is a right angle in the mix.
It is not a finished turn in the mix.
\end{observation}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{W1_heading.png}
\caption{Sheet W1. The bind, the cut, and the stations the kernel recorded. Station at $(1,0)$, $h=0.002$. Hits lie on $y=0$.}
\label{fig:plan}
\end{figure}

% ======================================================================
\section{The hit}
% ======================================================================

\begin{definition}[Hit]
A heading starting at \((1,0)\) is walked by repeated steps.
A hit is a step at which the heading's second coordinate changes sign.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[First gap]
\(H_0\) is the step index of the first hit.
\end{definition}

On the recorded run, \(H_0=1571\), side down, heading
\[
(-0.99999992,\; -0.00040316).
\]
The heading is on the far side of the line, one kick of vertical off it.

Stand on the line you count and the first event is the far side of that line.
Stand off the line and the first gap is only how far the start was from the wall.
This run stands on the diameter.

\begin{observation}
The distance from the start heading to the first hit heading is \(1.99999996\).
The distance from the first hit heading to the second is the same figure.
A heading exactly on the far side of the axis would give exactly \(2\).
The walk lands one kick of vertical short of that, every time, because a hit is a sign change and not a landing.
\end{observation}

% ======================================================================
\section{The pair and the lock}
\label{sec:pairlock}
% ======================================================================

After the first hit the scale of a hit is its index \(m=1,2,3,\ldots\).

\begin{definition}[Pair]
\[
r=m,\qquad \frac1r=\frac1m.
\]
\end{definition}

The pair is written on the integer \(m\).
It does not read the heading.
Once \(m\) is named, the walk is no longer required.

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:pair}
For every \(m\ge 1\),
\[
r\cdot\frac1r=1,\qquad
\mathrm{GM}\!\left(r,\frac1r\right)=1.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The product is immediate.
The geometric mean is \(\sqrt{r\cdot(1/r)}=\sqrt{1}=1\).
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Leftover]
\[
\tau(m)=\mathrm{AM}-\mathrm{GM}
=\frac{m+1/m}{2}-1.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:tau}
\[
\tau(m)=\frac{(m-1)^2}{2m}.
\]
\(\tau(m)=0\) if and only if \(m=1\).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\[
\frac{m+1/m}{2}-1
=\frac{m^2+1-2m}{2m}
=\frac{(m-1)^2}{2m}.
\]
The numerator vanishes only at \(m=1\).
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:tau-inv}
\(\tau(m)=\tau(1/m)\) for every \(m>0\).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Substitute.
\end{proof}

Hit \(m=1\) is the lock.
The pair is \((1,1)\).
The leftover is \(0\).
The count has moved once and has not yet had room to differ from its own inverse.

Hit \(m=2\) is the first pair that is two places: \((2,1/2)\), arithmetic mean \(5/4\), leftover \(1/4\).
Both ends now exist.

Nothing after the lock is a new kind of figure.
Every later \(m\) writes the same pair, the same product, the same leftover formula, at a larger \(r\).

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{D1_lock.png}
\caption{Sheet D1. The lock: pair $(1,1)$ at $m=1$. The bind is the pair.}
\label{fig:lock}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{D2_pair.png}
\caption{Sheet D2. The first two-place pair $(2,1/2)$ at $m=2$, on the home wall.}
\label{fig:pair}
\end{figure}

