\section{The outward point}
\label{sec:outward}
% ======================================================================

At every hit the pair is not only two numbers.
It is a point.
\[
P_m = m\,u_m,
\]
where \(u_m\) is the bound heading at that hit.
\(P_1=u_1\) is the lock itself.
Every later \(P_m\) is the same construction, walked out along a different ray, at a distance the count itself supplies.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{P1_plan.png}
\caption{Sheet P1. Plan of the model looking down the count. Outer locus $P_m=m u_m$ in ink; inverse locus $Q_m=u_m/m$ in the accent; bind of radius $1$; cut $y=0$.}
\label{fig:modelplan}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Four points on the line}
\label{sec:fourpoints}

At a hit the heading lies on the cut \(y=0\) to the residual of one kick.
Four points sit on that line, written as signed abscissae along the ray: the inner mark \(1/m\), the outer mark \(m\), the unit mark \(+1\), the opposite pole \(-1\).

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:harmonic}
For every \(m>1\),
\[
\left(\frac1m,\, m;\, 1,\, -1\right)=-1.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\[
\frac{(1-1/m)/(1-m)}{(-1-1/m)/(-1-m)}
=\frac{(m-1)/m}{1-m}\cdot\frac{-(m+1)}{-(m+1)/m}
=-1.
\]
\end{proof}

On every live row of the recorded run the value is \(-1\) to machine precision.
The four points are the pair and the two marks the diameter already fixed.
This is not a coincidence of this particular pair: any involution of a line with two fixed points sends every point to its partner in a constant cross-ratio of \(-1\) with those two fixed points \cite{coxeter1969}.
The map \(z\mapsto 1/z\) is exactly such an involution, fixing \(1\) and \(-1\); Proposition~\ref{prop:harmonic} is the instance the walk happens to write, not an exception to the rule.

A neighboring four on the same line,
\[
\bigl(m-1,\, m+1;\, m,\, 0\bigr)=\frac{m+1}{m-1},
\]
depends on \(m\), and does not tend to a fixed value as an involution's does; it grows toward \(1\) only in the sense of approaching it from above as \(m\to\infty\).
Anchor both reference points on a tick instead of the origin, and the dependence sharpens rather than fades:

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:tickorigin}
For every \(m>2\),
\[
\bigl(m-1,\,m+1;\,m,\,1\bigr)=\frac{m}{2-m}.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Direct evaluation of the cross-ratio formula; the \(m\) does not cancel because one reference point, \(1\), is fixed while the other, \(m\), moves with the count.
\end{proof}

The value tends to \(-1\) as \(m\to\infty\) --- the same limit Proposition~\ref{prop:harmonic} reaches exactly at every finite \(m\) --- but only ever approaches it here, never reaches it at any finite hit.
The lock against the pair is constant.
Ticks against a fixed point that is not the pair's own fixed point are not, and the rate at which they fail to be constant is exactly \(m/(2-m)\).

\subsection{Ticks on the generator}
\label{sec:generator}

A different four, built entirely from evenly spaced integers rather than from the pair, is constant for a different reason.

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:ladder}
For integers \(a\ne b\) and \(m>\max(a,b)\),
\[
\bigl(m-a,\,m+a;\,m-b,\,m+b\bigr)=\left(\frac{b-a}{a+b}\right)^{2}.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Direct evaluation; the \(m\) cancels in the four-term formula before the square, since all four points shift by the same \(m\).
\end{proof}

In particular,
\[
\bigl(m-2,\,m+2;\,m-1,\,m+1\bigr)=\frac19,
\]
checked exactly at \(m=3,4,10,50,1000\).
This is a different invariance than Proposition~\ref{prop:harmonic}'s.
The pair is constant because \(1\) and \(-1\) are the fixed points of the same map that sends \(m\) to \(1/m\).
The generator ladder is constant because shifting four evenly spaced points by a common amount is itself an isometry of the line, and cross-ratio does not see translations.
Two unrelated reasons produce the same word, ``constant,'' on two unrelated families of four points; Proposition~\ref{prop:tickorigin} is the reminder that a four which is neither an involution's orbit nor an evenly spaced ladder has no such protection.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{D5_four_points.png}
\caption{Sheet D5. The pair's harmonic four ($1/m$, $m$; $1$, $-1$) beside the generator's evenly spaced four ($m-2$, $m+2$; $m-1$, $m+1$), drawn on parallel rays at $m=10$.}
\label{fig:fourpoints}
\end{figure}

\subsection{The count pays with two integers}
\label{sec:twoint}

After the first hit the recorded run writes only two gap values: \(1570\) and \(1571\).
On \(127\) hits, \(1571\) occurs \(102\) times and \(1570\) occurs \(25\) times.
Mean gap \(1570.80315\).
Mean gap times the kick: \(3.14160630\).

The walk cannot land on one length, so it writes both neighbors.
This is the discrete case of a theorem conjectured by Steinhaus and proved independently by several authors studying the distribution of \(\{n\alpha\}\) for irrational \(\alpha\): the return times of a fixed rotation to an interval take at most two values, adjacent integers determined by the rotation's own angle \cite{sos1958,swierczkowski1958}.
The walk here is that rotation, and the interval is a half-turn.

A named comparison after the table exists:
\[
\pi=3.14159265\ldots,\qquad
\lambda:=\frac{\pi}{\arctan h}=1570.798421.
\]
Those names sit between \(1570\) and \(1571\).
\(H_0\cdot h=3.142\) is the first gap only --- one long step, coarser than the mean.

On this run \(\{\lambda\}=0.798421>1/2\), so the long letter dominates.
The remaining word of long and short gaps is twenty-five copies of LLLLS, then LL, because the step budget ended mid-word.
That five is not a second figure invented to describe the word; it is the denominator of the best simple approximation to \(1-\{\lambda\}=0.201579\), whose continued fraction begins \([0;4,1,24,\ldots]\) with second convergent \(1/5\).
A short gap fires, on average, once every five hits, because \(1/5\) is the closest simple fraction to the short gap's own frequency.
A different \(h\) writes a different fraction and a different period, and the period is computable from \(h\) before the walk is ever run.

\begin{observation}
\label{obs:exactclosure}
Choose \(h=\tan(\pi/n)\) for an integer \(n\).
Then \(\lambda=n\) exactly, and every gap after the first hit equals \(n\), with no second letter, checked to \(200{,}000\) hits at \(n=100\).
\end{observation}

The two-letter alphabet of this section is what the walk writes at almost every kick.
It is not forced at every kick.
At the exact mixes of Observation~\ref{obs:exactclosure} the walk closes on a single integer, the same way a rotation by a rational multiple of \(\pi\) closes a finite orbit instead of an infinite one --- a measure-zero set of kicks, but not an empty one.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{SCH1_integers.png}
\caption{Sheet SCH1. The two-integer schedule of the recorded run.}
\label{fig:sch1}
\end{figure}

\subsection{The heading ratchets and resets}
\label{sec:ratchet}

On a long hit the heading is off the axis by about a kick, then two, then three, then four.
On the next short hit it is put back.

\begin{observation}
On every short hit of the recorded run,
\[
\frac{|h_y|}{m}=\alpha,\qquad
\alpha\approx 3.15762\times 10^{-6},
\]
to the digits of the mean, from \(m=5\) through \(m=125\).
\end{observation}

Snapped to the event, the heading is always a horizon hit, by definition.
Held against the first gap as a ruler, it walks off that ruler until a short gap settles the account.
The two readings are two rulers on the same walk.

Call the gap between an exact landing on the axis and the heading the operators actually deliver the \emph{entrance residual}.
It shrinks as \(h\) shrinks and never reaches zero.

