\subsection{Chord and path}
\label{sec:chordpath}

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:chord}
If the heading at hit \(m-1\) is exactly opposite the heading at hit \(m\), then
\[
|P_m-P_{m-1}|=2m-1.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Opposite unit headings \(u\) and \(-u\) give
\[
\bigl|m(-u)-(m-1)u\bigr|=(m+m-1)|u|=2m-1.
\]
\end{proof}

The hypothesis is never exactly true, by the residual already named.
On the recorded run the chord at \(m=2\) is \(3.000000\), at \(m=3\) it is \(5.000000\), at \(m=4\) it is \(7.000000\).
The short-gap rows differ from \(2m-1\) by a few parts in \(10^6\), the same size as \(\alpha\).

\begin{proposition}[Radius cancels]
\label{prop:cancel}
Let the chord above be drawn across a half-turn, and let the path be the first-order displacement of a radius growing linearly across that half-turn, at rate \(h\) per step.
Then
\[
\frac{\mathrm{chord}}{\mathrm{path}}=\frac{2}{(\mathrm{gap})\cdot h}.
\]
\end{proposition}

The radius cancels.
The ratio does not depend on which wall is being read.
On the recorded run the two bands are \(2/(1570h)=0.63694\) and \(2/(1571h)=0.63654\).
A named comparison, \(2/\pi=0.63662\ldots\), sits between them.
The last row of this run is a long hit, so the last ratio is the lower band, not a leftover angle treated as a second constant.

The path used here is first-order.
It is not a summed displacement of \(P\).

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{SCH2_bands.png}
\caption{Sheet SCH2. Chord over first-order path, two bands, with $2/\pi$ between them.}
\label{fig:bands}
\end{figure}

\subsection{The leftover, summed}
\label{sec:tausum}

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:tausum}
\[
\sum_{m=1}^{N}\tau(m)=\frac{N(N+1)}{4}+\frac{H_N}{2}-N,
\]
where \(H_N=\sum_{m=1}^{N}1/m\).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Sum \(\tau(m)=(m+1/m)/2-1\) term by term: \(\sum m/2 = N(N+1)/4\), \(\sum(1/m)/2=H_N/2\), and \(\sum 1\) over \(N\) terms is \(N\).
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The wedge integral]
\label{prop:wedgeint}
\[
\int_1^N \tau(k)\,dk=\frac{N^2-1}{4}+\frac12\ln N-(N-1).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\(\tau(k)=k/2-1+1/(2k)\); integrate term by term from \(1\) to \(N\).
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The trapezoid residual]
\label{prop:wedge}
Write \(S(N)\) for the discrete sum of Proposition~\ref{prop:tausum}, \(I(N)\) for the integral of Proposition~\ref{prop:wedgeint}, and
\(T(N)=I(N)+\tfrac12\bigl(\tau(1)+\tau(N)\bigr)\) for the trapezoidal estimate built from it.
Let \(\gamma=\lim_{N\to\infty}(H_N-\ln N)\) be the Euler--Mascheroni constant.
Then
\[
S(N)-T(N)=\frac12\bigl(H_N-\ln N\bigr)-\frac14-\frac1{4N}
\qquad\xrightarrow[N\to\infty]{}\qquad
\frac{\gamma}{2}-\frac14 = 0.0386078\ldots
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\(\tau(1)=0\), so \(T(N)=I(N)+\tfrac12\tau(N)\).
Subtracting the closed forms of Propositions~\ref{prop:tausum} and~\ref{prop:wedgeint} term by term,
\[
S(N)-I(N)
=\left[\frac{N(N+1)}4+\frac{H_N}2-N\right]-\left[\frac{N^2-1}4+\frac12\ln N-(N-1)\right]
=\frac{N+1}4+\frac12\bigl(H_N-\ln N\bigr)-1.
\]
Since \(\tau(N)=(N-1)^2/(2N)=N/2-1+1/(2N)\),
\[
\frac12\tau(N)=\frac N4-\frac12+\frac1{4N}.
\]
Subtract this from \(S(N)-I(N)\):
\[
S(N)-T(N)=\left[\frac{N+1}4+\frac12(H_N-\ln N)-1\right]-\left[\frac N4-\frac12+\frac1{4N}\right]
=\frac12(H_N-\ln N)-\frac14-\frac1{4N}.
\]
Let \(N\to\infty\): \(H_N-\ln N\to\gamma\) by definition of \(\gamma\), and \(1/(4N)\to0\).
\end{proof}

A term-by-term Euler--Maclaurin expansion of \(\tau\) using only its derivatives at the two endpoints, \(\tau'(x)=\tfrac12-\tfrac1{2x^2}\), predicts a correction of \(\tfrac{B_2}2\bigl[\tau'(N)-\tau'(1)\bigr]\to\tfrac1{12}\cdot\tfrac12=\tfrac1{24}\) \cite{apostol1976}.
That reasoning is not wrong about the polynomial part of \(\tau\); it is incomplete about the part that is not local.
\(\tau\) carries a term \(1/(2x)\), and the discrete sum of \(1/m\) differs from its integral not by anything visible in the derivatives of \(1/x\) at \(1\) and \(N\), but by the Euler--Mascheroni constant --- a limit, not a local correction.
\(\gamma/2-1/4\) and \(1/24\) are close (\(0.0386\) against \(0.0417\)) and easy to mistake for one another at a single \(N\), which is exactly why the recorded run's own digits, not either asymptotic guess, are what this series has always deferred to.

On the recorded run, \(N=127\): the exact discrete sum is \(3939.712667\); the integral plus trapezoidal correction is \(3939.674062\); the difference is \(0.038605\).
Proposition~\ref{prop:wedge}'s limit, \(\gamma/2-1/4=0.0386078\ldots\), already agrees with that run to five figures at \(N=127\); \(1/24=0.041\overline6\) does not.
As stated in \emph{Before the proofs}, this sum is always taken from the discrete closed form of Proposition~\ref{prop:tausum}, never from a running total carried across many hits and left uncompared to anything --- and, now, never mistaken for the bare integral it is not.

\subsection{What every hit shares}

\begin{align*}
r\cdot(1/r)&=1,\qquad
\mathrm{GM}(r,1/r)=1,\qquad
\tau(m)=(m-1)^2/(2m),\\
\bigl(1/m,\, m;\, 1,\, -1\bigr)&=-1,\qquad
\bigl(m-2,m+2;m-1,m+1\bigr)=1/9,\\
P_m&=m\,u_m,\\
\text{opposite-wall chord (idealized)}&=2m-1,\\
\text{chord/path bands (first-order)}&=2/(\mathrm{gap}\cdot h),\\
\textstyle\sum_{m=1}^N\tau(m)&=N(N+1)/4+H_N/2-N.
\end{align*}
The alphabet does not grow.
Every later hit writes the same words at a larger scale.

% ======================================================================
\section{The other end}
\label{sec:otherend}
% ======================================================================

Section~\ref{sec:outward} built one point from the count: \(P_m=m\,u_m\), walking outward, one hit at a time.
The pair it came from had two halves.
Only one of them has been drawn.

\[
Q_m=\frac{u_m}{m}.
\]

Nothing is added to produce \(Q_m\) that was not already proved above.

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:qisinv}
\(Q_m\) is the inversion of \(P_m\) through the bind: \(Q_m = P_m/|P_m|^2\).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\(|P_m|^2=m^2\), so \(P_m/|P_m|^2 = m\,u_m/m^2 = u_m/m = Q_m\).
\end{proof}

\(P_m\) and \(Q_m\) sit on the same ray, through the same origin, at the same instant.
They are not two walks that happen to share a clock.
They are one walk, and the classical operation of inversion through the bind, applied to it.

\begin{proposition}[Power of a point]
\label{prop:power}
\(\mathrm{pow}(P_m)\cdot\mathrm{pow}(Q_m) = -\mathrm{pow}(P_m)^2/m^2\), where \(\mathrm{pow}(X)=|OX|^2-1\) is the power of \(X\) with respect to the bind.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\(\mathrm{pow}(P_m)=m^2-1\); \(\mathrm{pow}(Q_m)=1/m^2-1=-(m^2-1)/m^2\).
Their product is \(-(m^2-1)^2/m^2=-\mathrm{pow}(P_m)^2/m^2\), an immediate corollary of Proposition~\ref{prop:qisinv}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The inward point}

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:pq}
\(|OP_m|\cdot|OQ_m|=1\) for every \(m\ge 1\).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\(|OP_m|=m\), \(|OQ_m|=1/m\), since \(u_m\) has length \(1\).
This is \(r\cdot(1/r)=1\), read as a distance.
\end{proof}

At \(m=1\), \(P_1=Q_1=u_1\).
For \(m>1\), \(P_m\) is further from the origin than the unit circle, and \(Q_m\) is closer, by the same walk, read in the two directions its own pair already names.

\begin{proposition}[Length reading of the leftover]
\label{prop:taulength}
\[
\tau(m)=\tfrac12\cdot\mathrm{dist}(\text{unit circle},P_m)\cdot\mathrm{dist}(\text{unit circle},Q_m).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\(\mathrm{dist}(\text{unit circle},P_m)=m-1\) along the ray;
\(\mathrm{dist}(\text{unit circle},Q_m)=1-1/m=(m-1)/m\).
Their product, halved, is \((m-1)^2/(2m)\), which is \(\tau(m)\) by Proposition~\ref{prop:tau}.
\end{proof}

The leftover was defined above as a gap between two means.
It is also half the product of two lengths on the ray --- how far \(P_m\) has walked past the lock, times how far \(Q_m\) has walked back from it.
At the lock both lengths are zero and \(\tau\) is zero for the same reason twice.

\begin{observation}
\(Q_m\) requires no rule beyond the one that produced \(P_m\).
Both points exist at every hit, from the first.
Neither waits on the other to arrive.
\end{observation}

\(P_m\) grows without bound as the count runs.
\(Q_m\) shrinks toward the origin and never reaches it, since \(1/m>0\) for every finite \(m\).
The lock is the one hit at which asking the question either way gives the same answer.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{S1_section.png}
\caption{Sheet S1. Section $y=0$. Outer stations on $z=|x|$; inverse stations on $z=1/|x|$, which at this scale collapse toward the count-axis. They meet at $m=1$.}
\label{fig:section}
\end{figure}

\subsection{The inward wall}

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:innerchord}
Under the same idealization as Proposition~\ref{prop:chord} --- opposite headings at consecutive hits ---
\[
|Q_m-Q_{m-1}|=\frac{2m-1}{m(m-1)}.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\[
\left|\frac{u}{m}-\frac{-u}{m-1}\right|
=\left(\frac1m+\frac1{m-1}\right)|u|
=\frac{2m-1}{m(m-1)}.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:product4}
\[
(2m-1)\cdot\frac{2m-1}{m(m-1)}
=\frac{(2m-1)^2}{m(m-1)}
\;\longrightarrow\; 4
\quad\text{as }m\to\infty.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\[
\frac{(2m-1)^2}{m(m-1)}
=\frac{4m^2-4m+1}{m^2-m}
=4+\frac{1}{m^2-m}.
\]
\end{proof}

At \(m=2\) the product is \(4.5\).
On the recorded run, at \(m=127\), it is \(4.000062\).
The limit is not a value the walk equals at any finite hit.
The formula is exact algebra given the idealized chords; that those chords approximate the real, drifting ones is the entrance residual, carried forward unchanged.

\subsection{Solids from the same walk}
\label{sec:solids}

Stack \(P_m\) and \(Q_m\) by height \(z=m\), so the outward locus becomes the cone \(r=z\) and the inward locus becomes the funnel \(r=1/z\), with the bind stood up as the cylinder \(r=1\) between them, from \(z=1\) to \(z=N\).

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:volumes}
\[
\frac{V_{\mathrm{in}}}{\pi}=\frac{(N-1)^2}{N}=2\,\tau(N),
\qquad
\frac{V_{\mathrm{out}}}{\pi}=\frac{(N-1)^2(N+2)}{3},
\qquad
\frac{V_{\mathrm{out}}}{V_{\mathrm{in}}}=\frac{N(N+2)}{3}.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\(V_{\mathrm{cone}}=\tfrac{\pi}{3}(N^3-1)\), \(V_{\mathrm{cyl}}=\pi(N-1)\), \(V_{\mathrm{funnel}}=\pi(1-1/N)\).
\(V_{\mathrm{out}}=V_{\mathrm{cone}}-V_{\mathrm{cyl}}\) and \(V_{\mathrm{in}}=V_{\mathrm{cyl}}-V_{\mathrm{funnel}}\); both reduce by direct algebra to the stated forms, and \((N-1)^2/N=2\tau(N)\) by Proposition~\ref{prop:tau}.
\end{proof}

No third point is needed to reach this.
The leftover \(\tau(N)\), already read as a gap between means and as half a product of two lengths, is now also, up to a factor of \(2\pi\), the volume of the solid the inward walk alone sweeps out between the lock and hit \(N\).
Three readings of one number, none of them requiring anything the walk had not already produced.

On the recorded run, \(N=127\): \(V_{\mathrm{in}}/\pi=126^{2}/127=2\tau(127)\), and \(V_{\mathrm{out}}/V_{\mathrm{in}}=127\cdot 129/3=5461\).

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{O1_oblique.png}
\caption{Sheet O1. The same vertices, un-cut. Outer cone in ink; inverse funnel in the accent; bind as the count-axis.}
\label{fig:oblique}
\end{figure}

% ======================================================================
\section{Meetings}
\label{sec:meetings}
% ======================================================================

\(P_m\) and \(Q_m\) share an origin and a ray, by construction, at every hit.
What was not yet asked is whether they share anything else --- a circle, a place neither one owns alone.
This section finds two such places.
One is exact at every hit, with no idealization in it.
The other is exact only under the idealization already named, and the run confirms it to the precision that idealization allows.

\subsection{The circle on \(OP\)}
\label{sec:circleOP}

Let \(O\) be the origin and let \(C_m\) be the circle with diameter \(OP_m\): centre \(P_m/2\), radius \(m/2\).

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:circleOP}
\(C_m\) meets the unit circle at the two points
\[
X_\pm
=\frac1m\,u_m
\;\pm\;
\sqrt{1-\frac1{m^2}}\;Ju_m,
\qquad
Ju_m=(-h_y,h_x),
\]
and the orthogonal projection of either \(X_\pm\) onto the ray of \(u_m\) is exactly \(Q_m\).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
A point \(X\) lies on \(C_m\) iff \(|X-P_m/2|^2=(m/2)^2\), which expands, using \(|u_m|=1\), to \(|X|^2=X\cdot P_m\).
On the unit circle, \(|X|=1\), so the intersection satisfies \(X\cdot u_m=1/m\).
Writing \(X=(1/m)u_m+t\,Ju_m\) and imposing \(|X|=1\) forces \(t=\pm\sqrt{1-1/m^2}\).
The projection of either root onto the ray of \(u_m\) is \((X\cdot u_m)u_m=(1/m)u_m=Q_m\).
\end{proof}

Nothing was idealized to get this.
\(P_m\) is the recorded outward point at hit \(m\).
The circle it defines meets the unit circle at a point whose foot on the ray is exactly the recorded \(Q_m\).
On the recorded rows \(m=1,2,4,5,10,36,127\), the foot agrees with the declared \(Q_m\) to machine precision, and \(|X_+|=1\) to the same precision.
At \(m=1\) the two circles that could be built this way --- on \(OP_1\) and on \(OQ_1\) --- coincide, and \(X_+=X_-=P_1=Q_1=u_1\): the lock, as the one place a construction stops needing two cases.
This construction is not new: it is the standard ruler-and-compass construction of an inverse point --- construct the circle on the diameter joining the centre and the point, and its intersection with the given circle marks the inverse on the line between them \cite{coxeter1969}.
What is being read here is that construction, run on the outward point the walk itself supplies.

Thales puts a right angle at each \(X_\pm\).
The kite \(OX_+P_mX_-\) therefore has two right angles.
The other two are \(2\arccos(1/m)\) and \(2\arcsin(1/m)\).
At \(m=2\) the kite is \(90^\circ\), \(90^\circ\), \(120^\circ\), \(60^\circ\).
Scaled by the count,
\[
2m\arcsin\frac1m \;\longrightarrow\; 2.
\]
At \(m=127\) the value is \(2.000021\).
The unit wall-chord, recovered from the opposite vertex of the same two circles.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{D6_kite.png}
\caption{Sheet D6. The circle on $OP_2$ meeting the unit circle at $X_\pm$; the kite $OX_+P_2X_-$ with its two right angles. The foot of either chord on the ray is $Q_2$.}
\label{fig:kite}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Inversion of a collinear diameter}
\label{sec:generallemma}

The circle-on-\(OP\) construction inverts a single point.
A second question is what inversion does to a whole circle built from two points of the walk, when those two points and the origin happen to sit on one line.

\begin{proposition}[Inversion of a collinear diameter]
\label{prop:geninv}
Let \(C\) be a circle with diameter endpoints \(A,B\), and suppose \(O\), \(A\), \(B\) are collinear.
Under inversion through a circle centred at \(O\), the image of \(C\) is the circle with diameter endpoints \(\mathrm{Inv}(A)\) and \(\mathrm{Inv}(B)\).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The line through \(O,A,B\) is an axis of symmetry of \(C\), since any diameter is.
Inversion centred at \(O\) commutes with reflection across any line through \(O\): reflecting first and inverting after gives the same point as inverting first and reflecting after, since both act on each ray through \(O\) independently.
So \(\mathrm{Inv}(C)\) inherits that same axis of symmetry, which forces its centre onto that line.
\(\mathrm{Inv}(C)\) also passes through \(\mathrm{Inv}(A)\) and \(\mathrm{Inv}(B)\), themselves on that line.
A circle meets any line through its own centre in exactly two points, and those two points are automatically antipodal.
So \(\mathrm{Inv}(A)\) and \(\mathrm{Inv}(B)\) are forced to be \(\mathrm{Inv}(C)\)'s diameter endpoints.
\end{proof}

Two hits \(m,n\) supply such a collinear pair whenever their headings are, to the entrance residual, parallel or antiparallel --- same wall or opposite wall, at any separation, not only adjacent hits.

\begin{proposition}[The swap, in general]
\label{prop:swap}
Under the idealization that \(u_m\) and \(u_n\) are equal or opposite, inversion through the unit circle sends the diameter-circle of \(P_m,P_n\) onto the diameter-circle of \(Q_m,Q_n\).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Proposition~\ref{prop:geninv} with \(A=P_m\), \(B=P_n\), together with Proposition~\ref{prop:qisinv} identifying \(\mathrm{Inv}(P_m)=Q_m\) and \(\mathrm{Inv}(P_n)=Q_n\).
\end{proof}

Checked against the recorded run at same-wall pairs \((m,n)=(2,4),(5,13),(5,25),(10,50),(3,127)\) and opposite-wall pairs \((5,14),(5,26),(10,51),(3,126)\), predicted and actual inner centres and radii agree to between four and twelve significant figures in every case.
The precision is not uniform, and it is not supposed to be: it is governed entirely by how close \(u_m\) and \(u_n\) are to exactly parallel or antiparallel at that pair, which is the same ratchet-and-reset drift of \S\ref{sec:ratchet}, not a separate source of error.
Proposition~\ref{prop:swap} is not a fact about consecutive same-wall hits.
It is a fact about any two collinear points the walk produces, of which consecutive same-wall and consecutive opposite-wall hits are the two smallest and most easily checked cases.

\begin{proposition}[Clearance from the bind]
\label{prop:clearance}
For the same-wall pair \(m,m+2\), under the idealization above, the outer diameter-circle stands off the unit circle by exactly \(m-1\) on its near side and \(m+1\) on its far side.
The inner diameter-circle stands off it by exactly \(1-1/m\) on its near side and \(1-1/(m+2)\) on its far side.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The outer circle has centre at distance \(m+1\) and radius \(1\); its near and far points from the origin sit at \(m+1-1=m\) and \(m+1+1=m+2\), so their distances from the unit circle are \(m-1\) and \(m+1\).
The inner circle has centre at distance \((m+1)/(m(m+2))\) and radius \(1/(m(m+2))\); its near and far points sit at \(1/(m+2)\) and \(1/m\), so their distances from the unit circle are \(1-1/(m+2)\) and \(1-1/m\).
\end{proof}

The diameter-circle of \(P_mP_{m+2}\) does not meet the unit circle for \(m\ge 2\).
The diameter-circle of \(Q_mQ_{m+2}\) does not meet it for \(m>1\).
At \(m=1\) both touch at the lock, and Proposition~\ref{prop:clearance}'s clearances both fall to zero, consistently.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{D7_swap.png}
\caption{Sheet D7. Inversion swapping the outer diameter-circle of the same-wall pair $P_2,P_4$ onto the inner diameter-circle of $Q_2,Q_4$.}
\label{fig:swap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{What does not meet}
\label{sec:notmeet}

\(P_m\) grows without bound as \(m\) runs.
\(Q_m\) shrinks toward the origin and never reaches it.
The two readings share every hit and share no destination.

The chord product of the inward and outward walls approaches \(4\) and is not equal to it at any finite \(m\).
A meeting that is forced holds at every hit without qualification, as the circle on \(OP\) does.
A meeting that depends on an idealization holds only as well as the idealization does, as the swap of Proposition~\ref{prop:swap} does --- and that idealization's accuracy varies with which pair is chosen, not with how far apart they are.
Where the two readings end up together is not a question with a yes, because one of them does not end up anywhere at all.

A smaller mismatch belongs here too, because it is the same discipline at a different scale.
Stand the same-wall span \(|P_{m+2}-P_m|\) up by height \(z=m\), so that \(P_m\) and \(P_{m+2}\) become \((m,m)\) and \((m+2,m+2)\) in a plane cut through the count.
Looking down that count --- the plan --- the span between them is \(2\).
Cut open to that plane instead --- the section --- the same two points sit on a line of slope \(1\) and are \(2\sqrt2\) apart, not \(2\).
Both numbers are exact; neither is a rounding of the other.
A quantity can be genuinely invariant in the object and still read differently depending on which projection of it is taken, and the two readings above are not in tension for the same reason the plan and the section of any solid are not in tension: they are answers to different questions asked of the same figure.

% ======================================================================
\section{What stands}
% ======================================================================

Forced, at every hit, once the operators of \S1 are granted:
the shear \(T_h\) and the bind \(N\), with no angle in either;
a hit as a sign change on the diameter the heading stood on;
the pair \(r=m\), \(1/r=1/m\), product \(1\), geometric mean \(1\);
the leftover \(\tau(m)=(m-1)^2/(2m)\), zero only at \(m=1\), and its closed-form sum;
the point \(P_m=m\,u_m\) and the point \(Q_m=u_m/m\);
\(Q_m\) as the inversion of \(P_m\) through the bind, and every fact below as a consequence of that one classical operation applied to the outward walk;
their product \(|OP_m||OQ_m|=1\) and their power-of-a-point product;
the leftover, again, as half a product of two lengths, and again as a volume;
four points on the cut writing \(-1\);
the generator ladder writing \(1/9\);
the circle on \(OP_m\) meeting the unit circle at a point whose foot is \(Q_m\) --- the classical construction of an inverse point, run on the walk's own output, with no idealization, at every hit including the lock;
the general lemma that inversion of a circle whose diameter is collinear with the centre of inversion sends it to the circle on the images of that diameter's endpoints, forced for any two such points, not only ones the walk happens to produce.

Forced under the idealization that two hits share a parallel or antiparallel heading, and confirmed by the run at whatever precision that idealization allows for the pair chosen:
the outward and inward chords, their product converging to \(4\);
the swap of Proposition~\ref{prop:swap}, checked at separations from \(2\) to \(124\);
the exact clearances of Proposition~\ref{prop:clearance}.

Reported from the table, and compared after the table exists:
two gap integers, with a single-letter alphabet at the exact mixes of Observation~\ref{obs:exactclosure};
mean gap times kick sitting next to \(\pi\);
two first-order chord/path bands with \(2/\pi\) between them;
\(\alpha\) on short hits of this run;
the plan-and-section mismatch of the same-wall span.

Named: entrance residual, ratchet and reset, the inward point, the general lemma, the foot of the chord, the generator ladder.

Not granted:
an angle as an argument of either move;
an exact opposite heading at consecutive hits, only an approach to one;
a value of \(\pi\) or of \(4\) treated as reached rather than approached;
a second move, or any rule governing \(Q_m\) beyond the one that produced \(P_m\);
a meeting of the two readings' destinations;
a third point, where two already account for everything checked;
a running sum trusted over its own closed form.

The first sentence of the series is still the sentence.
Where is \(1\)?
It is the lock: the one hit where the pair meets itself, where \(P\) and \(Q\) are still the same point, and where the two circles of \S\ref{sec:meetings} are still one circle --- before the count has given any of them somewhere else to be.

